<span class="enc-snip">Munuza was a Berber governor of the Muslim conquerors of Iberia, based at Gijón in Asturias in the early eighth century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/munychia"><span class="enc-name">Munychia</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Munychia is a steep hill in Piraeus, the port of Athens, with a harbour of the same name below it.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/munyigumba"><span class="enc-name">Munyigumba</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Munyigumba was a chief of the Hehe who in the mid-19th century united many small chiefdoms of the southern highlands of present-day Tanzania by conquest and alliance…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/munza"><span class="enc-name">Munza</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Munza was a king of the Mangbetu in the Uele region of north-eastern Congo.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/munzo"><span class="enc-name">Munzo</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Munzo was the site of the royal court established by Kalala Ilunga, the founder of the Luba Empire.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/muqaddimah"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/muqaddimah.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Muqaddimah</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Muqaddimah is the introduction to the universal history of Ibn Khaldun, completed in its first form in 1377.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/muqan-qaghan"><span class="enc-name">Muqan Qaghan</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muqan Qaghan was ruler of the Gokturk Empire from 553 to 572.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/muqarnas"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/muqarnas.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Muqarnas</span><span class="enc-type">Term</span><span class="enc-snip">Muqarnas is a form of architectural ornament in Islamic architecture consisting of tiers of small niches, creating a honeycomb or stalactite effect in vaults and domes.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/muqattam-hill"><span class="enc-name">Muqattam Hill</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Muqattam Hill is a range of hills on the eastern edge of Cairo.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/muqrin-ibn-zamil"><span class="enc-name">Muqrin ibn Zamil</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muqrin ibn Zamil was a ruler of the Jabrid dynasty of eastern Arabia, which held al-Ahsa and the islands of Bahrain.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/muqti"><span class="enc-name">Muqti</span><span class="enc-type">Institution</span><span class="enc-snip">A muqti was a holder of an iqta, a revenue assignment granted to military officers in medieval Islamic states.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/mur-river"><span class="enc-name">Mur</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Mur (Mura) is a river about 450 kilometres long in central Europe.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/murad-beg-of-kunduz"><span class="enc-name">Murad Beg of Kunduz</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Murad Beg was the Qataghan Uzbek chief who built a powerful state from Kunduz in northern Afghanistan in the early 19th century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/murad-giray"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/murad-giray.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Murad Giray</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Murad Giray was khan of Crimea from 1678 to 1683.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/murad-i"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/murad-i.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Murad I</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Murad I was the Ottoman sultan from 1362 to 1389 who made the Ottoman state a major power in the Balkans and Anatolia.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/murad-ii"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/murad-ii.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Murad II</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Murad II was sultan of the Ottoman Empire from 1421 to 1451, with a brief abdication in 1444 to 1446.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/muradid-dynasty"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/muradid-dynasty.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Muradid dynasty</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Muradid dynasty ruled Tunisia as hereditary beys under Ottoman suzerainty from 1613 to 1702.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/murano"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/murano.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Murano</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Murano is a group of islands in the Venetian lagoon, about 1.5 kilometres north of Venice.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/murashu-archive"><span class="enc-name">Murashu archive</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Murashu archive is a collection of about 730 cuneiform tablets of a Babylonian family business, the house of Murashu, found at Nippur in 1893.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/murata-seifu"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/murata-seifu.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Murata Seifū</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Murata Seifū (1783–1855) was a samurai official of the Chōshū domain who led its Tenpō-era reforms from 1838.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/muravsky-trail"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/muravsky-trail.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Muravsky Trail</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Muravsky Trail was the main route used by Crimean Tatar raiders from the sixteenth to the eighteenth century to strike north into Muscovy.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/murchison-falls"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/murchison-falls.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Murchison Falls</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Murchison Falls is a waterfall on the Victoria Nile in north-western Uganda, where the river forces its way through a gap only a few metres wide before flowing into…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/murcia"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/murcia.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Murcia</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Murcia is a city in south-eastern Spain that formed part of al-Andalus during the medieval Islamic period.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/mures-river"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/mures-river.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Mureș (river)</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Mureș, Maros in Hungarian, is a river of Romania and Hungary about 760 kilometres long.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/muri-abbey"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/muri-abbey.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Muri Abbey</span><span class="enc-type">Institution</span><span class="enc-snip">Muri Abbey is a former Benedictine monastery at Muri in the Swiss canton of Aargau, founded in 1027 by Ita of Lorraine and Count Radbot as the family monastery of the…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/muria"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/muria.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Muria</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Muria are a Gondi-speaking Adivasi people of the Bastar region of Chhattisgarh, India.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/muridun"><span class="enc-name">Muridun</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Muridun, 'disciples', were the followers of the Sufi teacher Ibn Qasi in the Algarve in south-western al-Andalus.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/muriq-shpata"><span class="enc-name">Muriq Shpata</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muriq Shpata was an Albanian ruler of the Despotate of Arta in north-western Greece, succeeding Gjin Bua Shpata in 1399.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/murom"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/murom.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Murom</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Murom is a city in Vladimir Oblast, Russia, on the left bank of the Oka.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/muroma"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/muroma.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Muroma</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Muroma were a Finnic people of the middle Oka basin, mentioned in the Primary Chronicle among the peoples paying tribute to the Rus'.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/murong-bao"><span class="enc-name">Murong Bao</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Murong Bao was the second emperor of the Xianbei state of Later Yan and son of its founder Murong Chui.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/murong-chao"><span class="enc-name">Murong Chao</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Murong Chao was the second and last emperor of Southern Yan, ruling Shandong from 405 to 410.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/murong-chong"><span class="enc-name">Murong Chong</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Murong Chong was a prince of the deposed Former Yan house who led the Xianbei rising against Former Qin after the Battle of Fei River.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/murong-chui"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/murong-chui.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Murong Chui</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Murong Chui was a Xianbei prince of the Former Yan who won fame as a general but fled to the Former Qin in 369 to escape court intrigue.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/murong-de"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/murong-de.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Murong De</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Murong De was a Xianbei prince, younger brother of Murong Chui of Later Yan, and founder of Southern Yan.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/murong-huang"><span class="enc-name">Murong Huang</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Murong Huang was the Xianbei leader who founded the Former Yan, declaring himself King of Yan in 337.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/murong-hui"><span class="enc-name">Murong Hui</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Murong Hui was chief of the Murong Xianbei in western Liaoning from 285 to 333.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/murong-jun"><span class="enc-name">Murong Jun</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Murong Jun was the ruler of Former Yan from 348 to 360 and son of Murong Huang.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/murong-ke"><span class="enc-name">Murong Ke</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Murong Ke was a prince and general of the Former Yan, son of Murong Huang.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/murong-nuohebo"><span class="enc-name">Murong Nuohebo</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Murong Nuohebo was the last ruler of Tuyuhun, the Xianbei kingdom around Qinghai Lake, reigning as a vassal of the Tang dynasty and married to a Tang princess.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/murong-wei"><span class="enc-name">Murong Wei</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Murong Wei was the last emperor of Former Yan, succeeding his father Murong Jun as a child in 360.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/murong-xi"><span class="enc-name">Murong Xi</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Murong Xi was the last emperor of Later Yan, ruling from Longcheng in Liaoning from 401 to 407.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/murong-xianbei"><span class="enc-name">Murong Xianbei</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Murong Xianbei were a branch of the Xianbei nomads who settled in western Liaoning in the 3rd century and adopted farming and Chinese-style government.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/murong-yong"><span class="enc-name">Murong Yong</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Murong Yong was the last ruler of Western Yan, a Xianbei state of the Sixteen Kingdoms period in China.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/murray-maclehose"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/murray-maclehose.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Murray MacLehose</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Murray MacLehose was a British diplomat who served as governor of Hong Kong from 1971 to 1982, the longest tenure of any governor.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/murrough-obrien-1st-earl-of-thomond"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/murrough-obrien-1st-earl-of-thomond.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Murrough O'Brien, 1st Earl of Thomond</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Murrough O'Brien was the last Gaelic king of Thomond in western Ireland.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/murshid-quli-khan"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/murshid-quli-khan.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Murshid Quli Khan</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Murshid Quli Khan was a Mughal official appointed diwan (revenue minister) of Bengal by Aurangzeb.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/murshidabad"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/murshidabad.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Murshidabad</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Murshidabad is a city on the Bhagirathi river in West Bengal, India.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/mursili-i"><span class="enc-name">Mursili I</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Mursili I was an early king of the Hittites, remembered for his long-distance raid down the Euphrates around 1595 BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/mursili-ii"><span class="enc-name">Mursili II</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Mursili II was King of the Hittites from about 1321 to 1295 BCE, a son of Suppiluliuma I.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/murska-sobota"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/murska-sobota.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Murska Sobota</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Murska Sobota is the main town of Prekmurje, the region beyond the Mura river in north-eastern Slovenia, which belonged to the Kingdom of Hungary until 1919.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/murtaza-nizam-shah-i"><span class="enc-name">Murtaza Nizam Shah I</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Murtaza Nizam Shah I was sultan of Ahmadnagar in the Deccan from 1565 to 1588.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/murud-janjira"><span class="enc-name">Murud-Janjira</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">Murud-Janjira is an island fortress off the town of Murud on the Konkan coast of Maharashtra, India.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/murut"><span class="enc-name">Murut</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Murut are an Indigenous people of the interior of northern Borneo, mainly in south-western Sabah in Malaysia and neighbouring parts of Sarawak, Kalimantan and Brunei.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/muryeong-of-baekje"><span class="enc-name">Muryeong of Baekje</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muryeong was king of Baekje from 501 to 523 and restored the kingdom's strength after its retreat to Ungjin.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/murzuq"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/murzuq.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Murzuq</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Murzuq is an oasis town in the Fezzan region of south-western Libya.</span></a></li></ul>
